<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-01-29">
  <title>Thursday, Jan 29</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This is an outline of the topics we covered.
      You should not rely only on my notes.
      Make sure to take your own notes, and, if you miss a class, get notes from another student in class.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>The Constant Coefficient Case</title>

    <p>
      Consider a constant coefficient second order homogeneous linear differential equation:
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="equation" tag="star"> a y'' + by' + cy = 0 </mrow>
      </md>
      A common idea in solving certain differential equations is: guess-and-check works well for good guessers.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does <xref ref="equation"/> have a solution of the form <m>y = e^{rx}</m> for some <m>r</m>? If so, then:
          <md>
            <mrow> y' \amp = re^{rx} </mrow>
            <mrow> y'' \amp = r^2e^{rx} </mrow>
            <mrow> ar^2 e^{rx} + bre^{rx} + ce^{rx} \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> ar^2 + br + c \amp = 0 </mrow>
          </md>
          Using the quadratic equation:
          <md>
            <mrow> r \amp = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} </mrow>
          </md>
          We'll refer to <m>ar^2 + br + c</m> as the <term>characteristic polynomial</term> of <xref ref="equation"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the equation:
          <md>
            <mrow> y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0. </mrow>
          </md>
          Then the characteristic polynomial is <m>r^2 - 3r + 2 = (r - 1)(r - 2)</m>, so both <m>y_1 = e^x, y_2 = e^{2x}</m> are solutions.
          Since these are linearly independent, the general solution must be:
          <md>
            <mrow> y = C_1e^x + C_2 e^{2x}. </mrow>
          </md>
          Suppose we wanted to add initial condition information, such as <m>y(0) = 1</m>.
          Plugging this information into the general solution:
          <md>
            <mrow> y = C_1e^x + C_2 e^{2x} </mrow>
            <mrow> 1 = C_1 + C_2 </mrow>
          </md>
          This is not enough information to pin down a single solution.
          For a second order equation, an initial condition must include two pieces of information:
          <md>
            <mrow> y(x_0) \amp = y_0 \amp y'(x_0) \amp = y_0' </mrow>
          </md>
          If we also suppose that <m>y'(0) = -1</m>, then <m>y' = C_1e^X + 2C_2e^{2x}</m>, so:
          <md>
            <mrow>1 \amp = C_1 + C_2 </mrow>
            <mrow>-1 \amp = C_1 + 2C_2 </mrow>
          </md>
          Subtracting gives: <m>2 = 0 - C_2</m>, so <m>C_2 = -2</m>.
          Plugging back in to either equation gives <m>C_1 = 3</m>, so the unique solution satisfying the initial condition is:
          <md>
            <mrow> y = 3e^x - 2e^{2x}. </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider <m>y'' + 2y' + y = 0</m>, with characteristic polynomial <m>r^2 + 2r + 1 = (r+1)^2</m>.
          This has <m>-1</m> as a repeated root, so we have a solution <m>y_1 = e^{-x}</m>, but no second solution.
        </p>

        <p>
          Suppose we guess <m>y_2 = xe^{-x}</m>.
          We can easily check that this is a solution:
          <md>
            <mrow>y_2' \amp = e^{-x} - xe^{-x} = (1-x)e^{-x} </mrow>
            <mrow>y_2'' \amp = -e^{-x} - (1-x)e^{-x} = (x - 2)e^{-x} </mrow>
          </md>
          Plugging in to the differential equation:
          <md>
            <mrow> (x-2)e^{-x} + 2(1-x)e^{-x} + xe^{-x} \amp =  0 \checkmark </mrow>
          </md>
          Since <m>y_1, y_2</m> are linearly independent, we have the general solution:
          <md>
            <mrow> y = C_1e^{-x} + C_2xe^{-x}. </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      A quadratic polynomial must have either two real roots, one real repeated root, or a pair of complex conjugate roots.
      It remains to consider the last case.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The numbers <m>a + bi</m>  and <m>a - bi</m> are called <term>conjugate</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <fact>
      <title>Euler's Formula</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>e^{i\theta} = \cos(\theta) + i \sin(\theta).</mrow>
          </md>
          A slight variation, by plugging in <m>-\theta</m> and using the even/odd properties of <m>\cos/\sin</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>e^{-i\theta} = \cos(\theta) - i \sin(\theta).</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the equation <m>y'' - 4y' + 5y = 0</m>, with characteristic polynomial <m>r^2 - 4r + 5</m>.
          Using the quadratic equation:
          <md>
            <mrow> r = \frac{4 \pm \sqrt{16 - 20}}{2} = \frac{4 \pm \sqrt{-4}}{2} = 2 \pm i. </mrow>
          </md>
          So we find two solutions <m>y_1 = e^{(2 + i)x}, y_2 = e^{(2 - i)x}</m> which are complex-valued functions.
          Using Euler's Formula:
          <md>
            <mrow> y_1 \amp = e^{(2 + i)x} = e^{2x} \cos(x) + i e^{2x} \sin(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> y_2 \amp = e^{(2 - i)x} = e^{2x} \cos(x) - i e^{2x} \sin(x) </mrow>
          </md>
          Now, we can define new functions:
          <md>
            <mrow> y_3 \amp = \frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} = e^{2x} \cos(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> y_4 \amp = \frac{y_1 - y_2}{2i} = e^{2x} \sin(x) </mrow>
          </md>
          By the Superposition Theorem, these are also solutions to the differential equation.
          Since they are linearly independent (not constant multiples of each other), then the general solution to the equation is:
          <md>
            <mrow> y = C_1 e^{2x} \cos(x) + C_2 e^{2x}\sin(x). </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      To summarize: given <m>a y'' + by' + cy = 0</m>, we have three cases.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            With distinct real roots <m>r_1, r_2</m>, the general solution will be:
            <md>
              <mrow> y = C_1 e^{r_1x} + C_2 e^{r_2x}. </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            With a repeated real root <m>r</m>, the general solution will be:
            <md>
              <mrow> y = C_1 e^{rx} + C_2 xe^{rx}. </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            With complex conjugate roots <m>\alpha \pm i \beta</m>, the general solution will be:
            <md>
              <mrow> y = C_1 e^{\alpha x}\cos(\beta x) + C_2 e^{\alpha x} \sin(\beta x). </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>